    {
      "id": "hook",
      "title": "hook (one fixed spot)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Look at the dish on this roof. It's pointed at one fixed spot in the sky, and it never has to move. Here in Australia, dishes like this point north, towards the equator. Yet the space station races across the same sky in a few minutes. Every satellite has to keep moving to stay in orbit, so how can one seem to hang over the same spot all day? Have a think."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 3,
      "holdKind": "countdown",
      "planningResponseInterval": {
        "countdownSeconds": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "keep-pace",
      "title": "keeping pace with the ground",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "The satellite does move. The trick is that the ground under it moves too. Earth turns once a day, so if the satellite goes round once a day, in the same direction, it keeps pace with the spot below it. So the first requirement is a period equal to Earth's rotation period. That's actually twenty-three hours fifty-six minutes, because Earth turns once relative to the stars in that time, but most questions use twenty-four hours."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "period-radius",
      "title": "the period fixes the radius",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Last time, we saw that choosing the period chooses the radius. Rearrange r cubed over T squared equals big G M over four pi squared, and r cubed equals big G M T squared over four pi squared. With T as twenty-four hours, which is eight point six four times ten to the four seconds, r cubed is about seven point five seven times ten to the twenty-two. Take the cube root, and r is about four point two three times ten to the seven metres from Earth's centre."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "height-speed",
      "title": "how high and how fast",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Subtract Earth's radius, and the altitude is about three point five nine times ten to the seven metres, roughly thirty-six thousand kilometres up, more than five and a half Earth radii. Its speed is two pi r over T, about three point zero eight kilometres per second. That's much slower than the station's seven point seven, just as a higher orbit should be."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "equatorial-plane",
      "title": "why it must be over the equator",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "But the right height and period still aren't enough. Gravity pulls towards Earth's centre, so the plane of every orbit passes through Earth's centre. Tilt the orbit, and for half of each day the satellite is north of the equator, and for the other half it's south. From the ground, it would drift up and down the sky. That's called geosynchronous, but not geostationary. The only orbit that stays above one latitude all day is the one in the equator's plane."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "direction-circle",
      "title": "same direction, and a circle",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Two more conditions. It must travel west to east, the same way Earth turns, or it would pass overhead twice a day instead of staying put. And the orbit must be circular, so its speed stays constant and it doesn't drift ahead of the spot below, or fall behind it. The right period, the equator's plane, Earth's direction, and a circle. Only then is it geostationary."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "uses-costs",
      "title": "what it's good for, and the costs",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "That's exactly what fixed dishes need. Television and communications satellites sit there, so a dish can be bolted in place. Weather satellites use it too. Japan's Himawari satellite sits above the equator, north of Australia, and photographs the same half of the planet every ten minutes. There are costs, though. It's so far away that a signal up and back down takes about a quarter of a second. And near the poles, the satellite sits very low on the horizon, or can't be seen at all."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "near-earth",
      "title": "near Earth: the opposite trade",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Low orbits are the opposite. A satellite a few hundred kilometres up goes round in about an hour and a half, so it crosses your sky in minutes. Being close is great for detailed images of the ground, for a crewed station, and for internet with a short delay. But you need many satellites to cover the planet, and dishes that track them. In between, the navigation satellites behind GPS orbit about twenty thousand kilometres up, twice a day."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "worked-low-orbit",
      "title": "worked example: a low orbit",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Let's predict a low orbit. An Earth-imaging satellite flies seven hundred kilometres up, so r is seven point zero seven one times ten to the six metres. Using T equals two pi times the square root of r cubed over big G M, the period is about five thousand nine hundred seconds, roughly ninety-eight minutes. That's nearly fifteen laps a day. If its orbit passes over the poles, Earth turns beneath it, and each lap crosses a different strip of ground, so within days it can photograph almost the whole planet. It sees everywhere, but nowhere all the time."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "memory-aid",
      "title": "memory aid",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's a way to remember the geostationary conditions: one day, one plane, one way. One day for the period, the equator's plane, and Earth's direction of spin. Get those, plus a circle, and the height follows from Kepler's third law."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 2,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
